{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775351101-oYiEkNmiX69CVOtdWrn3","role":"assistant","created":1775351103,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I found the data for June 2003. The \"Grand total\" for June 2003 is $2,289,997 million (or about $2.29 trillion). Now I need to find the data for June 2013. Let me check the 2013 page.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_129edb5809fb4f8694a69a4a","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"cat /app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_09_page_62.txt"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
{"type":"message","message":{"id":"msg_896fe361-c390-4e8e-ab34-6a207d0b899d","role":"user","created":1775351103,"content":[{"type":"toolResponse","id":"call_129edb5809fb4f8694a69a4a","toolResult":{"status":"success","value":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"58\nCAPITAL MOVEMENTS\nCHART CM-A.—U.S. Liabilities to Foreigners Reported by U.S. Banks, Brokers, and Dealers with Respect to Selected Countries\n<table><tr><th colspan=\"6\">[In millions of dollars. Source: Treasury International Capital Reporting System]</th></tr><tr><th rowspan=\"2\">Country</th><th colspan=\"5\">Calendar years</th></tr><tr><th>2009</th><th>2010</th><th>2011</th><th>2012</th><th>June 2013</th></tr><tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>784,529</td><td>996,991</td><td>1,085,392</td><td>880,355</td><td>966,504</td></tr><tr><td>All other Europe</td><td>688,431</td><td>628,606</td><td>837,420</td><td>799,415</td><td>804,771</td></tr><tr><td>Caribbean banking centers 1</td><td>1,862,921</td><td>1,894,964</td><td>1,757,344</td><td>1,619,561</td><td>1,763,293</td></tr><tr><td>Japan</td><td>185,546</td><td>162,291</td><td>217,606</td><td>178,715</td><td>208,131</td></tr><tr><td>All other Asia</td><td>522,362</td><td>463,866</td><td>506,066</td><td>504,964</td><td>471,722</td></tr><tr><td>Subtotal</td><td>4,043,789</td><td>4,146,718</td><td>4,403,828</td><td>3,983,010</td><td>4,214,421</td></tr><tr><td>All other countries</td><td>436,178</td><td>464,402</td><td>511,105</td><td>538,279</td><td>550,437</td></tr><tr><td>Grand total</td><td>4,479,967</td><td>4,611,120</td><td>4,914,933</td><td>4,521,289</td><td>4,764,858</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"6\">1 Includes Bahamas, Bermuda, British Virgin Islands, Cayman Islands, Netherlands Antilles, and Panama.</td></tr></table>\nU.S. liabilities to foreigners reported by U.S. banks were recorded at $4.8 trillion in June 2013, an increase of $240 billion from year-end 2012. U.S. banking liabilities include foreign holdings of U.S. short-term securities but exclude foreign holdings of U.S. long-term securities. U.S. banking liabilities increased about $131 billion in 2010 and $304 billion in 2011 but decreased $394 billion in 2012.\nU.S. banking liabilities are concentrated in international financial centers. The data on this page show that about 57 percent of U.S. banking liabilities is currently recorded against the United Kingdom and banking centers in the Caribbean. Overall, banking liabilities rebounded in 2010 and 2011 from earlier declines, with the exception of Caribbean banking centers. In 2012, liabilities to Caribbean banking centers continued to fall and liabilities to other international financial centers and Japan turned down. However, liabilities to “all other countries” have continued to grow at a steady pace in the post crisis years. In the first two quarters of 2013, liabilities to most regions increased, especially those to the Caribbean banking centers and the United Kingdom. The only region to which there was a decline in liabilities was “all other Asia”.\nSeptember 2013","annotations":{"priority":0.0}}],"structuredContent":{"stdout":"58\nCAPITAL MOVEMENTS\nCHART CM-A.—U.S. Liabilities to Foreigners Reported by U.S. Banks, Brokers, and Dealers with Respect to Selected Countries\n<table><tr><th colspan=\"6\">[In millions of dollars. Source: Treasury International Capital Reporting System]</th></tr><tr><th rowspan=\"2\">Country</th><th colspan=\"5\">Calendar years</th></tr><tr><th>2009</th><th>2010</th><th>2011</th><th>2012</th><th>June 2013</th></tr><tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>784,529</td><td>996,991</td><td>1,085,392</td><td>880,355</td><td>966,504</td></tr><tr><td>All other Europe</td><td>688,431</td><td>628,606</td><td>837,420</td><td>799,415</td><td>804,771</td></tr><tr><td>Caribbean banking centers 1</td><td>1,862,921</td><td>1,894,964</td><td>1,757,344</td><td>1,619,561</td><td>1,763,293</td></tr><tr><td>Japan</td><td>185,546</td><td>162,291</td><td>217,606</td><td>178,715</td><td>208,131</td></tr><tr><td>All other Asia</td><td>522,362</td><td>463,866</td><td>506,066</td><td>504,964</td><td>471,722</td></tr><tr><td>Subtotal</td><td>4,043,789</td><td>4,146,718</td><td>4,403,828</td><td>3,983,010</td><td>4,214,421</td></tr><tr><td>All other countries</td><td>436,178</td><td>464,402</td><td>511,105</td><td>538,279</td><td>550,437</td></tr><tr><td>Grand total</td><td>4,479,967</td><td>4,611,120</td><td>4,914,933</td><td>4,521,289</td><td>4,764,858</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"6\">1 Includes Bahamas, Bermuda, British Virgin Islands, Cayman Islands, Netherlands Antilles, and Panama.</td></tr></table>\nU.S. liabilities to foreigners reported by U.S. banks were recorded at $4.8 trillion in June 2013, an increase of $240 billion from year-end 2012. U.S. banking liabilities include foreign holdings of U.S. short-term securities but exclude foreign holdings of U.S. long-term securities. U.S. banking liabilities increased about $131 billion in 2010 and $304 billion in 2011 but decreased $394 billion in 2012.\nU.S. banking liabilities are concentrated in international financial centers. The data on this page show that about 57 percent of U.S. banking liabilities is currently recorded against the United Kingdom and banking centers in the Caribbean. Overall, banking liabilities rebounded in 2010 and 2011 from earlier declines, with the exception of Caribbean banking centers. In 2012, liabilities to Caribbean banking centers continued to fall and liabilities to other international financial centers and Japan turned down. However, liabilities to “all other countries” have continued to grow at a steady pace in the post crisis years. In the first two quarters of 2013, liabilities to most regions increased, especially those to the Caribbean banking centers and the United Kingdom. The only region to which there was a decline in liabilities was “all other Asia”.\nSeptember 2013","stderr":"","exit_code":0},"isError":false}}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
